<!--
  Junction page § Live by default. Claims lifted from packages/junction/README.md
  (Channels, Browser client: real-time events and resource()), docs/internals.md
  § channels.ts (a broadcast is graded per recipient; a channel on the service
  covers a background write; changed is the announcement for a write that cannot
  name its row), src/client/index.ts (reconnect backoff, the reload on
  reconnect, FJS-701: the server queues nothing for an absent socket) and
  test/realtime-grading.test.ts.
  The proof is a real run, not a doc fence: the service below was started on a
  scratch port with an in-memory Litestone client, two Bun WebSocket clients
  joined the channel (one is the row's customer, one is another signed-in user),
  and the writes in OUT were made over HTTP and by a route that writes as the
  system. The frames are quoted from that run; the handshake frames (connection
  and connected) are left out.
  Not claimed: replay of what a client missed (there is none, the page says so),
  the announce() hook as the way to cover a job (the run showed a hook reaches
  service calls only; the channel declared on the service reached the job's
  writes too), Sierra's tethered() (a different package's page), and any
  delivery guarantee beyond the open socket.
  The samples sit in consts: a brace in markup opens a Mesa expression.
-->
<script>
  const SERVICE = `model Order {
  id         Int    @id @default(autoincrement())
  customerId Int
  status     String @default("pending")
  @@gate(1.4.4.5)
  @@allow(read, customerId == auth().id || auth().isAdmin)
}

app.services.register(createService({
  name: 'orders',
  model: 'Order',
  channel: 'orders',
}))

app.configure(channels(a => {
  a.channels.on('connection', (_session, conn) => a.channel('orders').join(conn))
}))`

  const OUT = `// two sockets on the channel: customer 5, and another signed-in user
POST /orders  (as admin)  ->  201
owner  {"type":"event","event":"orders created",
        "data":{"id":1,"customerId":5,"status":"pending"}}
other  (no frame)

// a route that writes as the system: one create, one bulk update
owner  orders created  {"id":2,"customerId":5,"status":"pending"}
owner  orders changed  {"model":"Order","operation":"updateMany","count":2}
other  orders changed  {"model":"Order","operation":"updateMany","count":2}

// the owner's socket is closed, one POST /orders, the owner reconnects
frames for the write made while away: none`
</script>

<section id="live" class="bc-section">
  <div class="container stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">Live by default</p>
    <h2>A write reaches every open screen, and nobody wrote the broadcast.</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Name a channel on the service and each write is pushed over the WebSocket to the
      clients allowed to read it. The browser client applies the push to the list on screen.
    </p>

    <div class="bc-split">
      <ul class="bc-points">
        <li>
          <strong>One line, not a pub/sub layer.</strong>
          <code>channel: 'orders'</code> on the service pushes every create, patch and
          remove as an event named for it, such as <code>orders created</code>. The only other
          code is the line that joins a connection to the channel.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A subscriber hears only what it may read.</strong>
          Both sockets sit on the same channel, and the row's read rule decides who gets the
          frame: in the run the customer got the row and the other user got nothing.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A job's write is announced too.</strong>
          A write made as the system reaches the same sockets, graded the same way. A bulk
          write names no row, so it sends a <code>changed</code> count, and a list reloads on it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A dropped socket reconnects, and it does not replay.</strong>
          The client backs off from one second to thirty and reloads a live list after the
          reconnect, because the server queues nothing for an absent socket. The write made
          while the socket was down never arrived as a frame; the reload is what catches it up.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <figure class="bc-proof">
        <pre class="code">{SERVICE}</pre>
        <pre class="code">{OUT}</pre>
        <figcaption>
          A real run of that service with two Bun <code>WebSocket</code> clients, on a scratch
          port with an in-memory Litestone client. The first write was a <code>POST</code>
          over HTTP. Abridged: the handshake frames are left out. The other user's
          <code>changed</code> frame carries a count and no row, and a reconnecting socket is
          not sent what it missed.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <p class="bc-more">
      Read more:
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/junction/README.md">channels and the browser client</a> ·
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/junction/docs/internals.md">how a broadcast is graded</a> ·
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/junction/test/realtime-grading.test.ts">the real-socket tests</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .bc-proof pre.code { overflow-x: auto; font-size: .82rem; margin: 0 0 .75rem; }
</style>
